\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces SoftWall\xspace  architecture. Both placements reach the coordinator through one host interface. (1) Each owner publishes its NeuralRx\xspace  outcome to a decision page. One common-cutoff batch then updates the debt table, the scheduler retimes retained debts around a candidate lease, and the independent verifier commits $X'$ or keeps $X_t$. (2) The coordinator publishes each action with its generation and (3) sends the lease with an absolute latest-start through the AI lease RPC. (4) A completion or non-launch marker retires the lease. The shared lane runs retained recoveries in certificate order. In (a), the processes on one GPU exchange IQ, LLR, and TB buffers through CUDA IPC rings. In (b), the evaluated multi-GPU placement uses NVLink P2P; cuPHY instance 0 owns TB1--TB3, instance 1 owns TB4 and TB5, and ellipses mark further TBs.}}{6}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overall}{{3}{6}{\sys architecture. Both placements reach the coordinator through one host interface. (1) Each owner publishes its \NRx outcome to a decision page. One common-cutoff batch then updates the debt table, the scheduler retimes retained debts around a candidate lease, and the independent verifier commits $X'$ or keeps $X_t$. (2) The coordinator publishes each action with its generation and (3) sends the lease with an absolute latest-start through the AI lease RPC. (4) A completion or non-launch marker retires the lease. The shared lane runs retained recoveries in certificate order. In (a), the processes on one GPU exchange IQ, LLR, and TB buffers through CUDA IPC rings. In (b), the evaluated multi-GPU placement uses NVLink P2P; cuPHY instance 0 owns TB1--TB3, instance 1 owns TB4 and TB5, and ellipses mark further TBs}{figure.caption.9}{}}
